 \section{Conclusion}
 Held-out economic utility, a construction-calculator comparator and recorded resources provide complementary evidence for evaluating numerical LLM decision services. Assigned reasoning budgets improve endpoint utility while leaving an aggregate calculator deficit; weighting, invalid-output denominators and task composition materially affect interpretation. Decision-sensitive extraction repair specifies a prospective extension with a conditional construction-regret certificate; its utility and cost effects remain untested. The design implications for deterministic calculation paths and financial workflows remain untested. Appendix incentive sensitivity clarifies compute assumptions without establishing observed strategic behavior or financial deployment.
