+\section{Executable admission and CPU reference contract}\label{app:admission}
+\textbf{Public source contract.} A frozen manifest supplies unique relevant record IDs, immutable source text, one currency, private value and the feasible menu. Inclusion must already be resolved publicly. The CPU profile interprets only a whole-record field of the form \texttt{record\_id=<ID>; price\_to\_beat=<amount> <currency>}, with a finite nonnegative decimal amount. It performs no currency conversion or implicit interpretation of opening bids, superseded drafts or withdrawals. Extra prose, multiple amounts and missing units remain unresolved. The declared schema must identify this task's construction price; syntax alone cannot establish factual truth.
+
+\textbf{Atomic admission.} A proposal names a manifest ID and exact amount offsets. The verifier independently checks the entire grammar, identity, units, amount and offsets before admitting a point interval. Wrong numbers, duplicate IDs, unsupported spans or nonnested updates reject the whole repair batch without altering prior intervals. Unknown records retain $[0,+\infty)$ and stay in the fixed denominator. No hidden prices, scoring labels or model confidence enter admission. The parser can read every admitted field without an LLM.
+
+\textbf{Execution boundary.} Exact rational arithmetic preserves inclusive thresholds and lowest-bid ties. Each attempted call reserves an output cap within the remaining allowance and consumes a call slot before execution; failures retain their charged tokens and recorded stage times. The reference has no model backend or GPU timing pilot. Fixtures check malformed inputs, interval updates, action bounds and exhausted caps, not real extraction or statistical coverage. Existing development traces use a different record-language protocol; adapting them would require a frozen adapter and independent cases. The companion contract documents all checks and preserves unresolved outcomes.
